WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.775
Robots and people perform countless different manipulations.

00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:08.850
Yet underneath, they share a handful of reusable skills.

00:00:09.350 --> 00:00:13.750
Can a vision-language model discover those skills, just by watching?

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.975
Here's the task. A model watches a stream of videos, one after another.

00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:25.350
It locates each manipulation in time, and writes it as a skill call.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:30.600
For every new event, it decides: reuse a skill from its library,

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:34.840
or create a new one.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.040
The library carries over, so every decision shapes what comes next.

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.440
Reuse too eagerly, and different skills collapse into one.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.840
Create too eagerly, and duplicates pile up.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:55.615
Video2Skill tests this on robot and human kitchen videos, with over twelve thousand verified skill calls.

00:00:55.915 --> 00:01:03.240
We tested 19 open-source VLMs. Many group events near chance, falling into exactly these two traps.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:10.665
Fine-tuning, including our CLaRe method, improves grouping, yet libraries stall below half the reference size.

00:01:11.265 --> 00:01:15.815
The open challenge: recognizing when existing skills are not enough.
